   "text": {
    "L1": "The Moon can look like a thin slice, a half-circle, or a full round Moon.",
    "L2": "Does the Moon really change shape? No. Its real shape stays round.",
    "L3": "The Moon does not make its own light. Sunlight falls on it and bounces toward us.",
    "L4": "Half of the Moon is always lit by the Sun. As the Moon goes around Earth, we see different amounts of that lit half.",
    "L5": "At Amavasya, the new Moon is not seen. After it, the lit part grows night by night.",
    "L6": "The shape grows from a thin crescent to a half-Moon, then to almost full and Purnima.",
    "L7": "From Amavasya to Purnima takes about 15 days. After Purnima, the lit part shrinks back toward Amavasya.",
    "L8": "One full cycle takes about 29 to 30 days. These phases are not caused by Earth’s shadow."
   },
